# Jarvis — model configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in what you have. `.env` is git-ignored; this
# file is committed and must never contain a real key.
#
# Read at boot from the working directory Jarvis was started in — so `npm run dev`
# and `npm run runtime` from this folder find it, and a packaged Jarvis launched
# from somewhere else finds nothing and comes up with no model rather than with
# someone else's. Anything already exported in the shell wins over this file.
#
# None of it is required. With no key and no local server, Jarvis plans missions
# with its built-in planner, says `planner: "deterministic"` on the wire, and shows
# "Planner: built-in" in Mission Control. That is a working Jarvis, not a broken
# one — what it will not do is pretend a model was consulted.
#
# Nothing here reaches the renderer. Keys stay in the runtime process; the window
# is told the provider name, the model name, and one line about how it was
# configured.

# --- Anthropic (recommended) -------------------------------------------------
# https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Only for a gateway or proxy that speaks the Messages API. Leave blank for the
# real thing.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=

# --- An OpenAI-compatible server ---------------------------------------------
# llama.cpp, LM Studio, Ollama's /v1 endpoint, vLLM, OpenRouter, or the paid API.
# A local server usually needs no key at all: set the URL and leave the key blank.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1

# --- Which one to use --------------------------------------------------------
# auto (default) | anthropic | openai-compatible | hermes | offline
#
# `auto` picks by what is present: an Anthropic key, then an OpenAI-compatible
# server, then the supervised Hermes agent if it is up and idle, then nothing.
# `offline` is how you turn the model off without deleting your key.
#
# The same choice can be made in config.json as `llmProvider`, which wins over
# this; config.json also holds `llmModel`, `llmBaseUrl`, `llmPlanner` and
# `llmTimeoutMs`. It never holds a key.
# JARVIS_LLM_PROVIDER=auto

# Overrides the default model. `claude-opus-5` for Anthropic; whatever your local
# server serves otherwise.
# JARVIS_LLM_MODEL=

# --- Embeddings for memory search (optional) ---------------------------------
# Similarity over what Jarvis remembers. With nothing set here it uses a local
# lexical hash: 256 dimensions over word and character n-grams, no network, no
# native module. That matches *wording*, not meaning — so it reports
# `semantic: false`, and the Memory page says so in those words rather than
# calling it semantic search.
#
# `OPENAI_BASE_URL` above is what switches this on: the same server serves both,
# and an embedding model is a different endpoint on it rather than a different
# key. A server that is unreachable leaves the existing index alone and reports
# itself degraded; it does not empty what was already indexed.
#
# The index holds an id, a change hash and a vector — never the text of a memory.
# JARVIS_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text-v1.5
